# A missing hashed asset must return a real 404, never the SPA shell.
#
# Cloudflare Pages auto-detects this project as a single-page app (index.html
# with no 404.html) and answers every unmatched path with index.html at status
# 200, Content-Type: text/html. For a module script that response is the
# classic "Expected a JavaScript-or-Wasm module script but the server responded
# with a MIME type of text/html" console error — and it is worse than cosmetic:
# older service-worker versions cached that HTML under the asset URL, leaving
# the device on a blank screen until the cache name was bumped.
#
# The trigger is mundane: a deploy swaps the whole hashed-asset set, and any
# tab still holding the previous shell asks for hashes the new deployment no
# longer contains. With a real 404 here, that moment degrades into a clean
# 404 (never cached — res.ok is false in the service worker, and browsers do
# not cache 404s) plus one app-level auto-reload (vite:preloadError handler
# in main.js) instead of a poisoned cache.
#
# Static assets always win over redirect rules, so existing files are served
# untouched; only genuinely missing /assets/* paths reach this rule.
/assets/* /404.html 404

# SPA deep links (vue-router history mode) still need the shell.
/* /index.html 200
